shadowfetch-defaults (2.1.4-1) umbra; urgency=high

  * Repair missing installed-system SSH host keys during 2.1.3 upgrades.
  * Include Debian's llama.cpp-services transition package in the signed
    retirement manifest so direct APT upgrades complete on their next boot.
  * Replace the Hermes and OpenClaw installers, launchers, documentation and
    health probes with a verified Buzz Desktop 0.5.8 and Relay 0.2.1 setup.
  * Add a rootless, loopback-only Buzz relay with owner-private generated
    secrets and a persistent user service.
  * Delegate open-model recommendation, download and serving to Buzz's native
    hardware-aware Compute workflow on the loopback-only port 9337; remove the
    duplicate Ollama, Open WebUI and Shadowfetch model-picker paths.
  * Verify the official Buzz amd64 package by release-pinned SHA-256, simulate
    its apt transaction, refuse removals and preserve an installed newer build.
  * Replace the Debian 550 NVIDIA fallback with NVIDIA's signed Debian 13
    repository and nvidia-driver-assistant hardware selection.
  * Move vendor systemd units and udev rules from administrator-owned /etc to
    /usr/lib, with conffile cleanup for 2.1.3 upgrades.
  * Depend on the current pkexec and polkitd packages instead of the obsolete
    policykit-1 transitional package.
  * Normalize Local AI launcher categories and remove an unused duplicate
    Control Center desktop file.
  * Add read-only Buzz shared-compute, loopback exposure and secret-permission
    checks to the system health and agent-doctor tools.
  * Keep the Buzz operation lock out of Podman child processes so relay stop,
    restart and upgrade operations cannot be blocked by a leaked descriptor.
  * Keep the hardware clock in UTC and enable systemd-timesyncd so a fresh
    install corrects its clock automatically instead of drifting across zones.
  * Retire the legacy Bedrock single-snapshot apt hook and let Phoenix use
    Debian's 80snapper hook as the sole owner of one pre/post recovery pair.
  * Make optional first-run app installs cancellable with bounded signal
    escalation, and reject unsafe root or parent-directory workspace paths.
  * Bridge upgrades from 2.1.3 through a strict second-generation Safe Update:
    recognize only the measured Plasma replacement pair, verify the package
    plan twice, and continue to refuse every unrecognized removal.
  * Retire the exact distro-managed 2.1.3 AI package set in the same Phoenix
    transaction, with a lock-aware next-boot fallback for direct APT upgrades;
    preserve personal workspaces and downloaded model data.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <shadowfetchlinux@gmail.com>  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 11:32:00 -0400

shadowfetch-defaults (2.1.3-1) unstable; urgency=high

  * Security/stability release.
  * First-boot setup no longer runs on a live session. shadowfetch-firstboot.service
    was gated only on firstboot.done, so booting the ISO to look at the distro ran
    the whole first-boot script on the visitor's machine -- setting the hardware
    clock to local time and fetching Flatpak themes before the firewall was even
    up. It now also requires the absence of /run/live/medium and /run/live/rootfs,
    the same guard already used for the GPU unit.
  * (See shadowfetch-branding 2.1.3-1 and the top-level release notes for the
    Calamares installer fix that shipped in this same release: sf-remove-live-user
    no longer signals processes by UID inside the target chroot.)

 -- Shadowfetch Project <shadowfetchlinux@gmail.com>  Sun, 02 Aug 2026 10:22:06 -0400
shadowfetch-defaults (2.1.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stability release. No feature additions.
  * Build is now reproducible: chroot and bootstrap mirrors are pinned to a
    snapshot.debian.org timestamp. Tracking "testing" meant a rebuild of 2.1.1 did
    not reproduce 2.1.1, and forky had already dropped network-manager-gnome, which
    the core package list declares -- an unpinned build aborted outright.
  * Encrypted installs can boot. sf-install-grub derived the target disk from
    grub-probe on /, which on LUKS returns /dev/mapper/luks-<uuid>; the trailing-digit
    strip then produced a device that does not exist and both grub-install calls
    failed. The disk is now derived from the ESP, with the old path as a fallback.
  * The live session no longer changes the visitor's machine or listens on port 22.
    First-boot setup is guarded off live media, so it no longer sets the hardware
    clock to local time on a machine nobody installed onto; ufw is enabled at build
    time instead of after a Flatpak download; and sshd is stopped and masked on live
    sessions, which shipped a published password with passwordless sudo.

 -- Shadowfetch Project <shadowfetchlinux@gmail.com>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 10:47:33 -0400

shadowfetch-defaults (2.1.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Correct the version the system reports about itself. The 2.1.0 image
